==============================================================================
SMALL-CORE SAFE-ARC FLOOR:  min over 12-subsets A of W(A) vs threshold 1/(7V)
==============================================================================

[1] min W over 12-subsets of {1..13}:
    min_A W(A) = 5/1848 = 0.002706  at A = (1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 7, 8, 9, 10, 11, 12, 13)

[2] min W over 12-subsets of {1..16} (allowing a moderate non-parked runner):
    min_A W(A) = 5/1848 = 0.002706  at A = (1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 7, 8, 9, 10, 11, 12, 13)

[3] If small core A=S\{V} has W(A) >= w0 = 0.002706,
    then C holds via V as soon as  V > 1/(7 w0) = 52.8000.
    i.e. for V >= 53, C is AUTOMATIC via the parked runner.
    => only parked runners V <= 52 need a separate check (FINITE).

[4] adversarial 12-core: large incommensurate runners to MINIMIZE W (bounded)
    min W over adversarial 12-cores: 5/1848 = 0.002706
      at A = (1, 2, 3, 5, 7, 8, 9, 11, 12, 13, 50, 53)
    NOTE: each runner contributes total danger measure exactly 2*(1/14)=1/7 (u teeth x 2c/u).
    With 12 runners, total danger <= 12/7 (overlaps reduce it); a gap can still close.

INTERPRETATION:
  The floor argument bounds W from below ONLY when the 12-core is the SMALL set {1..13}.
  When the other 12 runners include large incommensurate ones, W CAN be driven small.
  BUT in a PRIMITIVE covering 13-set the small runners 1..13 are forced to be present
  to cover small moduli (you cannot cover q=11,13 etc with only large multiples and stay
  13 runners), so the relevant cores are NOT freely adversarial. That is the gap C closes.
